{"name":"Midday Sun","hex":"#ffe1a3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"midday","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Noon; twelve o'clock during the day."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/midday"},{"term":"sun","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Sundanese."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The star that is closest to the Earth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sun"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mid-day Sun","blocks":[{"text":"Mid-day Sun (1934–1954) was a British Thoroughbred racehorse and sire. After showing little promise as a two-year-old in 1936, Mid-day Sun improved into top class performer at three. In 1937 he won five races including The Derby and the Hardwicke Stakes at Royal Ascot. He was retired to stud in 1938 but had little success as a stallion. He was the first winner of the Derby at Epsom to be owned by a woman."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Mid-day_sun.jpg/330px-Mid-day_sun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mid-day_Sun","wikidata":"Q6840855"},{"title":"Noon","blocks":[{"text":"Noon is 12 o'clock in the daytime."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/%D0%91%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%83._%D0%A3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8C._1861.jpg/330px-%D0%91%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%83._%D0%A3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8C._1861.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noon","wikidata":"Q168182"},{"title":"Sun","blocks":[{"text":"The Sun is the star located at the centre of the Solar System. It is a massive sphere of hot plasma, heated to incandescence by nuclear fusion reactions in its core, radiating the energy from its surface mainly as visible light and infrared radiation, with 10% at ultraviolet energies. It is the main source of energy for life on Earth. The Sun has been an object of veneration in many cultures and a central subject of astronomical research since antiquity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun","wikidata":"Q525"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Solar_System_True_Color_EN.png/500px-Solar_System_True_Color_EN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Solar_System_True_Color_EN.png/1280px-Solar_System_True_Color_EN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Solar_System_True_Color_EN.png/500px-Solar_System_True_Color_EN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Size comparison of major celestial objects in the Solar System, including the Sun. Distances are not to scale.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASolar_System_True_Color_EN.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Highest_resolution_photo_of_Sun_%28NSF%29_as_of_January_20%2C_2020.jpg/500px-Highest_resolution_photo_of_Sun_%28NSF%29_as_of_January_20%2C_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Highest_resolution_photo_of_Sun_%28NSF%29_as_of_January_20%2C_2020.jpg/1280px-Highest_resolution_photo_of_Sun_%28NSF%29_as_of_January_20%2C_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Highest_resolution_photo_of_Sun_%28NSF%29_as_of_January_20%2C_2020.jpg/50